WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.254
A science fair team says its study helper is accurate because it answered nine questions correctly.

00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:13.819
Before celebrating, ask what those questions covered, what it missed, and whether the mistakes mattered.

00:00:13.819 --> 00:00:21.948
As you watch, keep one question in mind: A useful test is designed around the real job and the people affected by a wrong answer.

00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:24.762
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:24.762 --> 00:00:32.016
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:32.566 --> 00:00:36.951
A test score is like a weather forecast judged on one sunny afternoon.

00:00:36.951 --> 00:00:43.591
It tells you something about that moment, but not how the forecast handles a storm or a different season.

00:00:43.591 --> 00:00:47.098
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:47.098 --> 00:00:53.926
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:53.926 --> 00:00:56.682
Separate training, validation, and testing.

00:00:56.682 --> 00:01:00.190
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:05.498
The same system is tried again with longer questions and unfamiliar wording.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.563
Its score drops.

00:01:06.563 --> 00:01:13.074
That does not make the first result fake; it shows that the first test covered only a narrow situation.

00:01:13.074 --> 00:01:14.326
Pause for a moment.

00:01:14.326 --> 00:01:17.895
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:17.895 --> 00:01:21.839
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:23.968
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:29.540
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:36.714
Imagine a reminder tool that gets almost everything right but misses the one announcement about a deadline.

00:01:36.714 --> 00:01:39.933
A small count of errors can hide a big consequence.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.275
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:43.275 --> 00:01:49.156
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:49.156 --> 00:01:51.570
Calculate accuracy on a small dataset.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.284
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:55.284 --> 00:01:59.370
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:07.958
Write down the conditions before testing: who will use the tool, what inputs it will receive, and which mistakes would be costly.

00:02:07.958 --> 00:02:12.756
Then try fresh examples and record the misses as carefully as the successes.

00:02:12.756 --> 00:02:17.055
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:17.055 --> 00:02:20.607
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:26.464
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:27.014 --> 00:02:30.904
If a tool does well on short questions, what would you try next?

00:02:30.904 --> 00:02:36.861
Include a question with a different wording and one whose answer is not in the supplied material.

00:02:36.861 --> 00:02:40.690
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:47.376
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:50.537
Describe overfitting and a real-world testing plan.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:54.974
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:55.524 --> 00:02:57.756
Two tools each get eight out of ten.

00:02:57.756 --> 00:03:02.779
One makes two harmless formatting mistakes; the other invents two science facts.

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:06.003
The matching score does not make their risks match.

00:03:06.003 --> 00:03:07.988
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:07.988 --> 00:03:10.778
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:10.778 --> 00:03:16.049
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:19.025
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:19.025 --> 00:03:23.676
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:24.226 --> 00:03:29.645
Now open the practice activity, Accuracy is not the whole story, when you are ready.

00:03:29.645 --> 00:03:33.644
In the testing activity, look beyond the headline percentage.

00:03:33.644 --> 00:03:38.998
Notice which cases count as an error and what someone should do after finding one.

00:03:38.998 --> 00:03:41.643
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:41.643 --> 00:03:45.772
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:45.772 --> 00:03:49.642
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:50.192 --> 00:03:53.256
Your lesson project is called Write a testing plan.

00:03:53.256 --> 00:03:58.303
For your project, name the evidence that would make you pause or redesign the idea.

00:03:58.303 --> 00:04:03.530
A trustworthy plan can include a limit, another test, or a person who reviews results.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:08.097
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:08.097 --> 00:04:09.899
What would they need to know?

00:04:09.899 --> 00:04:13.143
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:13.143 --> 00:04:16.448
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:16.998 --> 00:04:22.081
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:22.081 --> 00:04:24.813
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:24.813 --> 00:04:28.308
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:28.308 --> 00:04:32.120
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.902
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:38.452 --> 00:04:45.421
Here is one idea to take with you: A useful test is designed around the real job and the people affected by a wrong answer.

00:04:45.421 --> 00:04:50.124
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:50.124 --> 00:04:54.374
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:54.374 --> 00:04:59.020
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.970
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:07.823
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:07.823 --> 00:05:13.617
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:13.617 --> 00:05:18.827
A useful test is designed around the real job and the people affected by a wrong answer.

00:05:18.827 --> 00:05:21.109
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:21.109 --> 00:05:24.914
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
